<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common dispute on cardamom shipments is size-grade mix — a lot sold as "Bold" that, on arrival, contains a meaningful share of smaller capsules below the 8mm threshold. This usually isn't intentional misrepresentation so much as inconsistent sorting; cardamom sizing is done by mechanical sieve and hand-sorting, and sorting discipline varies a lot between suppliers. Ask for a certificate of analysis stating the actual size-grade breakdown of your specific lot, not just a grade name, and specify a maximum tolerance for off-size capsules in your purchase order.</p>
<p>The second issue is colour and aroma degradation from poor storage before shipment — cardamom's volatile oils, which carry both aroma and much of its value, break down with heat and light exposure. A lot that was genuinely bright green and aromatic at harvest can arrive dull and faded if it sat in poor storage conditions before export. We store in cool, dark, airtight conditions specifically to prevent this, and it's worth asking any supplier directly how long their stock has been held and under what conditions before you order.</p>
<p>Third, moisture content above spec causes both weight disputes and accelerated quality loss in transit — cardamom at higher moisture is heavier (so you're effectively paying freight on water) and degrades faster once bagged. Confirm the moisture ceiling in your certificate of analysis, not just as a verbal assurance.</p>
<p>What first-time Gulf buyers most often get wrong: treating "cardamom" as a single product with one price, when size and colour grade alone can move the price several multiples apart. Specify your grade precisely before requesting a quote, and be skeptical of any quote that doesn't ask you for a size/colour spec first.</p>
